{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel at 3405b31b27c6aa70e206e9ff69d2f51ef82f3b7d. The sequencer carries a bounded submission queue and names its overload refusal.\n\nWHAT IS HERE. Options.MaxQueueDepth bounds how many submissions may be inside Submitter.Submit at once. Over the bound a submission is refused rather than queued, wrapping the sentinel ErrBackPressure (\"sequencer at capacity\"), taken at kernel.go:139 before the signed intent is parsed, before admission hooks, before the payload tree is written and before anything is chained onto the sequence ref. A zero limit leaves the queue unbounded, which is the embedding opt-out for a program using the kernel directly.\n\nWHAT THE SENTINEL ACTUALLY COVERS, because it is easy to read too narrowly. ErrBackPressure is returned from two places in submit, and this is deliberate rather than an oversight: the capacity refusal at :139, and the exhausted CAS retry limit at :538 after contention while chaining. The doc comment at :30-36 states both, and TestExhaustedRetryLimitIsBackPressureNotAnonymousFailure pins the second. The intent is that a caller can treat \"overload, try later\" as one condition with errors.Is and tell it apart from a malformed or unauthorized submission.\n\nThe consequence a reader must not miss is that only the first of the two is free. A capacity refusal leaves no object behind; a retry exhaustion has already decoded the intent and written objects. I checked this specifically because I was about to write documentation asserting that a refused submission costs almost nothing, which is true of the capacity refusal and false of the sentinel taken as a whole. The documentation in this same head says so explicitly rather than generalising from the cheaper case.\n\nRotate keeps its own anonymous exhaustion, errors.New(\"CAS retry limit exceeded\") at :398, matching what main already carried. It is a rare operator action, and proving its contention would need a production seam existing only for a test.\n\nTESTS at this exact head. TestSubmitRefusesAtCapacityBeforeChaining parks the first submission at the before_ref_cas failpoint and bounds its arrival with a ten-second select rather than sleeping. TestZeroMaxQueueDepthLeavesTheQueueUnbounded queues four followers, not one, so the unbounded case cannot pass by accident with a single waiter.\n\nGATES, all at this commit, uncached: go vet clean, go build clean, ./internal/kernel/... 131.778s, ./internal/app/... 36.381s, and -race on ./internal/kernel/... 94.192s.\n\nWHY THIS COMMIT EXISTS. It re-cuts the same tree onto main 27603774 under request 48c709bd, whose Rests-On trailer it carries. The previous head a86f35e1 could not merge: its limits.md still cited the retired f6c96085 and, once the citation repair landed, conflicted with main in that file. Recorded at assert b0413456.\n\nIt rests on the live non-stale predecessor at this exact path, d97eed89, which main carries.","body":{"commit":"3405b31b27c6aa70e206e9ff69d2f51ef82f3b7d","path":"internal/kernel"}}